### The java.util.Comparator Interface

A **`Comparator<T>`** is a comparison function that imposes a *total ordering* on some collection of objects. Unlike `Comparable`, which is implemented inside the class, a `Comparator` is an external object passed to sorting methods (`Collections.sort(list, comp)`, `list.sort(comp)`, or `new TreeSet<>(comp)`):
```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface Comparator<T> {
    int compare(T o1, T o2);
}
```

---

### The Modern Java 8+ Fluent Comparator API

Historically, writing comparators required verbose anonymous inner classes. Java 8+ introduced rich static and default factory methods that enable readable, type-safe declarative comparator pipelines:

```java
// 1. Basic property comparator via method reference:
Comparator<Flight> byPrice = Comparator.comparingDouble(Flight::getPrice);

// 2. Chained multi-level sorting:
Comparator<Flight> multiSort = Comparator
    .comparingDouble(Flight::getPrice)            // Primary: Lowest price first
    .thenComparingInt(Flight::getDurationMinutes) // Secondary: Shortest duration
    .thenComparing(Flight::getAirline);           // Tertiary: Airline alphabetical

// 3. Reversing order:
Comparator<Flight> expensiveFirst = byPrice.reversed();
```

---

### Null-Safe Sorting: nullsFirst & nullsLast

Sorting collections containing `null` elements or objects whose fields may be `null` naively throws a `NullPointerException`. Java provides built-in null decorators:
```java
// Treats null elements as smaller than non-null elements (placed at the beginning):
Comparator<String> safeAsc = Comparator.nullsFirst(String::compareToIgnoreCase);

// Treats null elements as larger than non-null elements (placed at the end):
Comparator<String> safeDesc = Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.reverseOrder());
```

---

